      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=f(x^2)\\\\f(g(x))&=\\sqrt{9-x^2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe domain of $g$ requires $x\\ge2$. The square root also requires\n$$\\begin{align*}9-x^2&\\ge0\\\\x^2&\\le9,\\end{align*}$$\nso $-3\\le x\\le3$. Both restrictions must hold. Therefore,\n$$\\operatorname{Dom}(f\\circ g)=[2,\\infty)\\cap[-3,3]=[2,3].$$\n\nAs $x$ runs through $[2,3]$, $x^2$ takes every value in $[4,9]$, so $9-x^2$ takes every value in $[0,5]$. Thus $\\sqrt{9-x^2}$ takes every value in $[0,\\sqrt5]$. The endpoints are attained:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(2))&=\\sqrt{9-4}=\\sqrt5\\\\f(g(3))&=\\sqrt{9-9}=0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, the range is $[0,\\sqrt5]$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
